# SYNOPSIS

```usage
uuencode [-m] [file] name
```

# DESCRIPTION

`uuencode` encodes <file>, or standard input if there is none or it is `-`, as lines of printable ASCII on standard output, which uudecode(1) turns back into the file.
The first line is `begin`, the file's permissions in octal and <name>, the name uudecode will give the file.
Each line after it holds 45 bytes of the file; a line of a backtick and a line `end` close it.

: `-m`
  Use Base64 instead: the header is `begin-base64`, each line holds 60 characters, and the last line is `====`.

`uuencode` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/uuencode`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/uuencode`.
There is no native `uuencode`.

# SEE ALSO

uudecode(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/uuencode.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A <file> that cannot be opened or read is reported as `uuencode: fopen` <file>`:`, or `'`<file>`' read error:`, and the error; these, a write error and the usage message exit 1.

# BUGS

`uuencode -` <name> writes `-` in the header, not <name>.

With standard input, the mode in the header is whatever the input is, such as a pipe.

`uuencode` is untested on VectraOS.
